<% content_for :title, "Overriding a component — shadcnrb" %>

<div class="space-y-8">
  <div class="space-y-2">
    <%= sui.h1 "Overriding a component" %>
    <%= sui.lead do %>
      After install you own every file under <%= sui.code "app/components/shadcnrb/" %>.
      Edit freely — this is the shadcn philosophy. For one-off tweaks, reach
      for a style class override; for a site-wide alternate look, build a
      <%= sui.link_to "named style", docs_styles_path, variant: :underline %>.
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%= sui.separator %>

  <div class="space-y-3">
    <%= sui.h2 "Adding a variant" %>
    <%= sui.p do %>
      Each component's <%= sui.code "Style" %> class returns its variant
      lookup table. Add a key and it's available via <%= sui.code "variant:" %>:
    <% end %>
    <% code = <<~RUBY
      # app/components/shadcnrb/button/style.rb
      module Shadcnrb
        module Button
          class Style
            def variants
              {
                default:         "bg-primary text-primary-foreground ...",
                destructive:     "bg-destructive text-destructive-foreground ...",
                outline:         "border border-input bg-background ...",
                outline_primary: "border border-primary text-primary hover:bg-primary/10",
                ghost:           "hover:bg-accent hover:text-accent-foreground"
              }
            end
          end
        end
      end
    RUBY
    %>
    <%= sui.codeblock code, syntax: :ruby %>
    <%= sui.codeblock DocsHelper::OVERRIDING_VARIANT_USE_SNIPPET, syntax: :erb %>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-3">
    <%= sui.h2 "Rewriting the markup" %>
    <%= sui.p do %>
      For anything beyond class strings — a different element tree, new
      attributes, extra slots — edit <%= sui.code "component.rb" %> directly.
      The file is yours; the gem only re-copies it on
      <%= sui.code "shadcnrb:install --force" %>.
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <div class="space-y-3">
    <%= sui.h2 "When to use a style instead" %>
    <%= sui.p do %>
      An ad-hoc variant or copy tweak stays in the component's own
      <%= sui.code "Style" %> class. When you want a whole alternate look —
      rounded-none + thick borders + shadow offsets — reach for a
      <%= sui.link_to "named style module", docs_styles_path, variant: :underline %>
      so it's toggleable and lives alongside the default.
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>
